course: books
lesson: 85
title: Just So Stories, How the First Letter Was Written, part 2: the picture goes wrong and the whole Tribe comes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Taffy finishes her picture, proud of it, and asks if the Stranger-man understands. He nods hard, but he is sure it means the great Chief is surrounded by enemies with spears. He races off for help without even asking the way, and Taffy sits down very pleased and tells her Daddy only that it is a surprise.
explain: By chance the Stranger-man finds Teshumai at her Cave, having lunch with other Stone Age ladies. He politely hands her the bark. She takes one look, screams, and the ladies knock him down and sit on him in a long line of six, while Teshumai pulls his hair.
explain: Teshumai reads the picture as a horror: Tegumai full of spears, Taffy's hair standing on end in fright, and a pack of people creeping up behind, which are really Taffy's beavers. The ladies fill the poor man's hair with mud, bang the tribal drums and call every chief of the Tribe.
explain: The Stranger-man is thumped, rolled on pebbles and called rude names, but he leads them all back to the river. There they find Taffy making daisy-chains and Tegumai happily spearing small carp with his mended spear.
explain: The whole Tribe has come, chiefs and all, armed to the teeth, shouting and frightening every fish for twenty miles. Teshumai hugs Taffy, and the Head Chief grabs Tegumai by his top-knot feathers and shakes him.
fact: Taffy draws «very hard and rather scratchily» and puts the spear into the Stranger-man's hand in the picture «just to remind you that you're to bring it».
fact: The way in the picture: two trees, a hill, the beaver-swamp (only the beavers' heads), then the Cave, which she drew too big because she «can't draw things very small».
fact: Taffy says Mummy is «the most beautifullest Mummy there ever was», and draws the spear outside the Cave though it is really inside, «in case you forget».
fact: The Stranger-man thinks the Chief «pretended not to notice me» because enemies were hiding in the bushes; he «raced off into the bushes like the wind».
fact: Taffy calls her plan «a little berangement of my own», her way of saying arrangement, and makes her Daddy promise to be surprised.
fact: Teshumai: «It's as plain as the nose on this Stranger-man's face»; this means it's very clear, but she is quite wrong.
fact: What Teshumai sees: Tegumai with his arm broken, a spear in his back, a man ready to throw a spear, another throwing a spear from a Cave, and «a whole pack of people», which are «Taffy's beavers really».
fact: The Neolithic ladies sit on the Stranger-man «in a long line of six», fill his hair with mud and beat «the Reverberating Tribal Drums» (reverberating = booming and echoing).
fact: The Tribe plans to make him lead them to where «he had hidden poor Taffy» before punishing him; the story is a comedy and nobody is really hurt in the end.
fact: Kipling lists the Tribe's chiefs with grand, funny titles borrowed from many lands, «Platoffs with their Platoons, and Dolmans with their Detachments», and says «Aren't those beautiful words, Best Beloved?»
fact: Tegumai says: «Why, the whole dear, kind, nice, clean, quiet Tribe is here, Taffy», a joke, because they are loud and armed.
fact: Real life: picture signs are still misread today. The same emoji can mean different things to different people, which is one reason we use words when it matters.
fact: Real life: signs made only of pictures work when the message is very simple, like a road sign or a toilet door, but they cannot say exactly «please bring the black-handled spear that hangs over the fireplace».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «it's giving» = it looks like, it has the feel of; «mardy» (Midlands) = sulky; «yeah nah» (Australia/New Zealand) = no; «craic» (Ireland) = fun, what's going on.
exercise: Why does Taffy draw the spear in the Stranger-man's hand? | To remind him that he is to bring it | Because he is holding a spear in real life, ready to throw | To show he is an enemy | She could not fit it anywhere else
exercise: In Taffy's picture, what comes after the two trees? | A hill | The Cave | The beaver-swamp | The river
exercise: Why did Taffy draw the Cave as high as the hills? | She can't draw things very small | It really is as high as the hills | Mummy asked her to | To make it easy for him to find
exercise: Where is the black-handled spear really kept? | Inside the Cave, over the fireplace | Outside the Cave, leaning on the wall | In the beaver-swamp | At the Head Chief's cave
exercise: What does the Stranger-man think the picture shows? | The great Chief surrounded by enemies, needing his tribe to help | A happy family at home, with a nice dinner of carp | A map to a hidden treasure | A spear for sale
exercise: Why does he think the Chief pretended not to notice him? | He fears his enemies are hiding in the bushes and watching | The Chief is too busy | The Chief is deaf | The Chief does not like strangers from far tribes and never speaks to them
exercise: How does the Stranger-man leave? | Races off into the bushes like the wind | Walks slowly, asking Taffy the way | Paddles off down the Wagai river in a little canoe | Stays to help mend the spear
exercise: What does Taffy call her plan when her Daddy asks? | «A little berangement of my own» | «A secret-s'prise» | «A picture-letter for Mummy, in the shape of a map» | «A great invention»
exercise: Where does the Stranger-man find Teshumai? | At the door of her Cave, with other ladies at lunch | Down at the river, washing hides | Asleep inside the Cave | Picking berries in the woods on the far side of the hill
exercise: How does Teshumai know who he has come from? | She doesn't; he finds her Cave by chance | Taffy's name is written on the bark in big letters | He stops at every cave on the way and asks for the Chief's wife | Tegumai sends a drum message
exercise: What do the Neolithic ladies do to the Stranger-man first? | Knock him down and sit on him in a line of six | Give him lunch and a drink of water | Tie him to a tree with reindeer-sinews and leather strips | Chase him into the swamp
exercise: What does Teshumai do while the ladies sit on him? | Pulls his hair | Reads the bark aloud | Runs to the river | Beats the drums
exercise: «It's as plain as the nose on this Stranger-man's face.» What does Teshumai mean? | It's very clear | His nose is plain | She can't see well | It's a hard puzzle
exercise: What does Teshumai think Taffy's sticking-up hair means? | That Taffy was badly frightened | That Taffy forgot to brush it | That Taffy is wearing feathers | That the wind was blowing hard all day by the river
exercise: What are the «whole pack of people» coming up behind Tegumai really? | Taffy's beavers | Fishermen from the river | The Stranger-man's tribe | The hills behind the Cave
exercise: What do the ladies put in the Stranger-man's hair? | Mud | Feathers | Beads and shells from the river | Leaves
exercise: How do the ladies call the Tribe together? | They beat the Reverberating Tribal Drums | They light a great signal fire on the hill | They send Taffy | They blow a shell
exercise: What is Taffy doing when the Tribe arrives? | Making daisy-chains | Crying by the river | Drawing another picture on a fresh bit of birch-bark | Swimming with the beavers
exercise: And Tegumai? | Spearing small carp with his mended spear | Still mending his broken spear with sinews and bee's-wax | Asleep on the bank | Hiding in the bushes
exercise: Taffy says: «Well, you have been quick! But why did you bring so many people?» Whom is she talking to? | The Stranger-man | Her Mummy, who is hugging her | The Head Chief of the Tribe | Her Daddy, Tegumai
exercise: How much fish is frightened away by the Tribe? | Every fish for twenty miles | Only the big carp | None at all | Every fish in the beaver-swamp
exercise: What does the Head Chief do to Tegumai? | Shakes him by his top-knot feathers | Hugs him tightly and kisses him on both cheeks | Gives him a new spear | Pushes him into the river
test: Leo: «Mum read the picture right, it's giving map vibes, she just overreacted.» (it's giving = it looks like) Put it right. | She read it wrong: she thought Tegumai was speared and enemies were coming | Right, she knew it was a map and only screamed for fun | She never saw the picture | She read it as a lunch invitation
test: Why is Tegumai's line «the whole dear, kind, nice, clean, quiet Tribe is here» a joke? | The Tribe is noisy, armed and has just frightened every fish away | The Tribe really is very quiet and kind, so he is simply being polite | He is talking about the beavers | It is a song
test: «Platoffs with their Platoons, and Dolmans with their Detachments.» Why does Kipling list such names? | For the fun of grand-sounding words, as he says himself | Because they were real Stone Age chiefs whose names are written in old history books | To teach army rules | To scare the reader
test: Josh, a bit mardy (sulky): «Stranger-man just ran off and nicked the bark.» What did he really do with it? | Ran miles to the Cave and politely handed it to Teshumai | Threw it into the river on the way home to his own far tribe | Kept it for his daughter | Burned it
test: What would have saved all the trouble? | Sending someone who could talk their language with the picture | Drawing the beavers bigger | Sending the picture by drum | Waiting until the Stranger-man had learned to read
test: Which is TRUE about the Stranger-man? | Though scratched and puffed, he still tries to be polite | He runs away from the Tribe | He understands every word the ladies say | He brings the black-handled spear
test: Molly from Dublin: «what's the craic with the mud?» (craic = what's going on) Why did the ladies put mud in his hair? | They thought he had hurt Tegumai and Taffy | It was a welcome custom | It was to keep him cool after his long, hot run through the brambles | He asked them to
test: Real life: which is a picture-sign that works well today? | A road sign showing children crossing | A picture-letter asking for one exact spear | A drawing that means «it might rain» | A picture of a feeling
test: Real life: Sam sends a face-with-tears emoji, and his gran thinks he is sad. What does that show, just like Taffy's picture? | A picture can be read in different ways | Gran can't see | Emojis always mean exactly the same to everyone | Sam is sad
test: Looking back: in part 1, why did the Stranger-man think Taffy was a «very wonderful child»? | She touched his magic tooth and did not swell up or burst | She could speak his language better than he could himself | She caught a carp | She drew well
test: Looking back: who did the Stranger-man think was the great Chief «Man-who-turns-his-back-on-callers»? | Tegumai, busy mending his spear | The Head Chief | Teshumai | Taffy
test: Looking back: what is the river called, where Tegumai and Taffy fish? | The Wagai | The Limpopo | The Amazon | The Thames
test: Looking back: in the Armadillo story, Painted Jaguar got muddled by words; here the Tribe gets muddled by what? | A picture | A rhyme | A song | A smell
test: Looking back: what did Taffy draw to show the way through the swamp? | The beavers' heads | Stepping stones | A rope across the water | A boat
test: Charlie from Auckland: «they found Taffy hurt and crying, yeah nah?» (yeah nah = no) What is right? | No: she was happily making daisy-chains | Yes, she was hurt by the enemies | Yes, she had fallen into the beaver-swamp and was soaking wet | She was hiding in a tree
test: ★★★ The ladies sit on the Stranger-man «in a long line of six». With Teshumai pulling his hair, how many women are on or at him? | Seven | Six | Five | Twelve
test: ★★★ Careful: who in this part understands the picture exactly as Taffy meant it? | Nobody | Teshumai | The Stranger-man | The Head Chief
test: ★★★ Taffy drew the spear OUTSIDE the Cave though it hangs INSIDE. Why does this make the misunderstanding worse? | It looks like a spear being thrown at someone from the Cave | It makes the Cave look empty | It hides Mummy behind the spear so nobody can see her | It looks like a tree
exam: Identify the activity in which Teshumai Tewindrow was engaged upon the arrival of the Stranger-man. | Entertaining other Neolithic ladies at lunch | Mending a spear in the back-cave | Fishing in the Wagai river in company with her husband and daughter | Beating the Tribal Drums
exam: Ascertain the true nature of the figures interpreted by Teshumai as «a whole pack of people». | They were beavers drawn by Taffy to indicate the route through the swamp | They were members of the Tribe of Tegumai, assembled in hierarchical order behind their Head Chief | They were enemies of the Tewaras | They were fish
exam: It is given that the Tribe arrived at the river. Select the response most consistent with what was there observed. | Taffy making daisy-chains and Tegumai spearing small carp | Tegumai lying wounded upon the bank | A great battle in progress | The beavers of the swamp attacking the Cave of Tegumai
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the limitation of picture-writing demonstrated in this part? | A picture does not fix its meaning in words, and may be misunderstood | A picture can be read only by Head Chiefs, Hetmans and other persons of rank | A picture is always understood by mothers | A picture requires birch-bark of sufficient size
